[05:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Amazing. &gt;&gt; Yeah. Let\'s talk about the actual mechanic that allows Signal to run in this loop. Uh so, here in Hyper Agent, we call it live mode. On a cadence, every 60 minutes in this case, Signal gets this same prompt in the same'

[05:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thread. So, it maintains all that context, and it sets the instructions for what we need it to do. It\'s got some overall operating principles. It\'s got um you know, language that I always want it to use or always want it to avoid,'

[05:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but then it\'s got these details about how how go about sourcing new prospects that are going to we\'re going to be reaching out to outbound, how to qualify leads that are inbound, uh how to update CRM, how to update us in Slack. I did'

[05:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'not obviously have to sit down and write this. Signal &gt;&gt; Yeah, I was going to ask you that. &gt;&gt; Exactly. Yeah, like I would never do that. Signal just wrote its own live mode prompt based on the job description essentially that I gave it.'

[06:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Got it. &gt;&gt; So this solar panel company is just one example. I want to show you a couple other things that &gt;&gt; uh high-bridging customers are doing in their sales interactions that would never have been practical to do before. So we have a landscaping company that is'

[06:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'creating these before and after transformation proposals just based on an inbound lead that comes with a picture of the customer\'s yard. So you get this before picture and then'

[06:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'based on the design practices of that firm and the actual products they use, they\'re able to generate this before and after shot. And it\'s all fully costed for that customer to uh react to or'

[06:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'respond to or tweak as a quote. And you can actually sell the vision now rather than needing to just kind of hand wave about, "Oh, this is kind of what it will look like." Or if you do did want to'

[06:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'visualize it before, you\'d have to spend the time in some really specialized software to create that visual. &gt;&gt; Yeah, I feel like I\'m at the the desk with Chip and Joanna Gaines showing me what my house is going to look like when they make it look very nice. And'

[07:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this is amazing in terms of the scale that you can bring it to uh the what you said, getting the customer right where they want to be. Their desire is to see their after phase. So their lawn after this looks like and granted, you\'re'

[07:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'looking at the vision, but you get that very quickly and in a way that they clearly know how to contact you, they know the price, and they understand exactly what you\'re going to do in the service that you provide because the'

[07:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'agent has all of that contacts. &gt;&gt; Exactly. Yeah, whether you\'re selling landscaping, solar panels, self-cleaning, um I was doing another one with the team at Airtable where they\'re doing this outbound motion where'

[07:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they wanted to embed a demo video that\'s super specific to that prospect. So, the approach we came up with there for a software product was, well, what if we actually had our agent sort of recreate'

[07:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the UI in a faithful way, but a stylized way? &gt;&gt; Yeah. &gt;&gt; So, that &gt;&gt; I built it &gt;&gt; you could create these demo videos like this. It can even add voiceover. So, this is one I did for Jensen Wong just for fun. But, you can see'

[08:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'uh if I play this here &gt;&gt; image attached. Then, field agents work across the entire grid. &gt;&gt; It\'s got the actual faithful recreation of the Airtable UI, but with a use case that\'s totally specific to just this'

[08:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'buyer. So, it\'s not like a generic demo video for anybody. That\'s the kind of stuff that you can do now with this agent. &gt;&gt; Amazing. &gt;&gt; Okay, so I mentioned that Signal has access to our Gmail inbox. It is actually able to reply directly right'

[08:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here. So, this looks like it\'s coming from me cuz it\'s just in my inbox, but Signal is actually able to qualify this lead as part of its live mode prompt and send that email. It can also just hold things in draft for me if uh I would'

[08:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prefer to review that before it goes out. &gt;&gt; And are there times when you have to nudge Signal and say like, "Hey, maybe this isn\'t exactly what I want." or this isn\'t exactly the prompt. Where do you do that at and how does that work?'

[09:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Yeah, exactly. So, you can do that directly in that main live mode thread. You can also do it over in Slack. So, a lot of our customers will deploy their agents to Slack and you can see here uh that I had a little thread with Signal.'

[09:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'In this case, I was just telling it to run the sprint, but that could be kind of incremental feedback or new instructions or things that I wanted to add to that live mode loop. And I can @mention Signal right here, give it that instruction, and then it will report'

[09:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'back. It can also proactively post to Slack without being prompted or without being mentioned. And so, you have humans prompting agents, but you also have agents prompting humans. If there\'s something that needs my attention, Signal can post about it here. You can'

[09:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'see I\'ve got some other agents hanging out in this Slack channel, as well. And then finally, I don\'t know about you, but I\'ve never met uh salesperson that likes updating CRM. Anytime I\'ve done anything sales-adjacent, it\'s like the'

[09:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'last thing I ever want to do. Uh Signal never gets tired of updating CRM both for its own benefit and yours to keep track of every interaction it\'s ever had. So it will keep track of all of the outbound campaigns and sprints that it\'s'

[10:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'doing, the accounts, all the assets that it\'s created, every activity and touchpoint as part of that live mode prompt, it gets updated in CRM. This is Airtable here, but it could be HubSpot, it could be Salesforce, whatever CRM'

[10:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re using. &gt;&gt; And I really like the activities part because it lets you audit your agent, know what it\'s doing, when it\'s acting, and like what actions it\'s taken. So you can ask it, "What did you do last week?" or "Tell me all the things that you'

[10:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'updates you made last week." and it can go back, read its logs, and understand these are all the runs that I made and the changes that I made, and where they were made. And so you have this system of record as well as the agent being able to take those actions. I think to me that\'s the best way to keep track of'

[10:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your agents is give them a log, understand what they\'re doing every time they wake up, and then you can go back and help teach it a little bit too. If you\'re like, "Hey, I didn\'t really like this way this this turned out." or "I really really like this. How do we turn'

[10:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this into a skill?" which is my favorite part of like, I don\'t know, you can even do that. So. &gt;&gt; It\'s helpful to have that log outside of just the red contacts and even outside of the skills. Like the skills are more like the verbs and actions and how, but'

[11:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the log can just be that history of everything that it\'s done, and it can go back and search that if needed. So that could be in Airtable if it\'s more structured data, that could just be a big markdown file, that could be a file'

